<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1621">1621</a>–<a href="/years/1683">1683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a>, was an English statesman who served Charles II as Chancellor of the Exchequer and Lord Chancellor. One of the eight Lords Proprietors of Carolina, he drafted the Fundamental Constitutions of Carolina with his secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>. He later led the campaign to exclude the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James, Duke of York</a>, from the succession, helping to found the Whig party, and died in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthony-ashley-cooper-1st-earl-of-shaftesbury.jpg" alt="Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Greenhill · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthony_Ashley-Cooper,_1st_Earl_of_Shaftesbury_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
